=== DEFECT FOUND AND FIXED ===

A RAISING STATE RESOLVER DESTROYED THE ENTIRE CYCLE.
The readiness check called resolve_type unguarded, so a resolver fault
propagated out of _invoke, out of _execute and out of run_cycle: the whole
cycle record was discarded, work that had already run became invisible, and
remaining work was never attempted. Identical in shape to the failure-store
defect fixed at T01.6.3, and forbidden for the same reason -- a fault in the
readiness check must never be the reason a cycle disappears.

Fixed by guarding the check and FAILING CLOSED: if readiness cannot be
determined, the engine does not run. v2 4.12's stage-order violation is what
happens when an engine runs on inputs that are not ready, and an unanswerable
check cannot establish that they are. The item is rejected with the reason
stated, the cycle continues, and the fault is not misattributed to an engine.
KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit still propagate.

=== SPECIFICATION NOTES ===
No document conflicts found. v2 4.12, 4.13, 5.5, IOM 2.5/2.6, N-14, N-17, N-18
and N-6 agree on every binding point.

One APPARENT tension, resolved without inventing anything: N-17 says
Orchestration is "directive, not reactive" and "does not react to object
availability". That governs what work is PLANNED -- the caller plans. This task
REJECTS invalid work, which is v2 4.12's explicit "enforce stage ordering"
responsibility. Validating a given plan's inputs is not selecting work. N-17
itself binds T01.6.5, which confirms the two are complementary.

TWO OPEN QUESTIONS DELIBERATELY NOT CLOSED:
  OQ-10 (stage skipping)  -- open, scheduled P6. Partial and stage-skipping
                             work sets are NOT rejected.
  OQ-11 (backflow)        -- open, scheduled T07.3.8. Reverse-ordered and
                             arbitrarily ordered work sets are NOT rejected.
Enforcing either would close an open question by implementation, phases early.
Both are asserted as non-behaviour by test and by mutation M26.

Ambiguity A1 (must inputs be ACTIVE, or merely exist?) was failed closed to the
criterion's literal word: existence is enforced, status is REPORTED via
input_statuses so a caller may apply its own rule. No ratified source states a
status precondition for invocation.
